	"Mini Protocol: Domain Ideation": {
		"scope": "markdown",
		"prefix": "p-protocol-ideate-domain",
		"body": [
			"<task>",
			"Collaboratively explore the domain and converge on a pragmatic model useful for the stated problem.",
			"</task>",
			"",
			"<context>",
			"Problem or process: ${1:describe the domain problem or process}",
			"Domain language and exact meanings: ${2:supply known terms and definitions}",
			"Relevant types, processes, documentation, or tests: ${3:supply references when available}",
			"Known business rules and invariants: ${4:supply known rules}",
			"Constraints, disagreements, and uncertainty: ${5:none known}",
			"</context>",
			"",
			"<instructions>",
			"- Treat this as a multi-turn modeling session. Do not attempt a complete domain model in the first response; establish the first useful language, rule, boundary, or invariant surface and let my feedback shape subsequent turns.",
			"- Ground proposed language and rules in supplied domain evidence; distinguish facts, assumptions, alternatives, and questions for a domain expert.",
			"- Use supplied domain names exactly. Surface overloaded, conflicting, or unstable terminology; never silently normalize it or substitute a convenient synonym.",
			"- Use DDD technical terms precisely. Do not label something an entity, value object, aggregate, bounded context, or domain event unless its defining characteristics are supported.",
			"- When repository references are supplied, inspect the relevant types and processes as evidence of the current implementation and model. Do not treat existing code as automatic domain truth or authority for future design.",
			"- Use strategic DDD concepts only where boundaries, ownership, or relationships materially affect the problem. Use tactical concepts only where behavior and invariants justify them.",
			"- Challenge leaky boundaries, anemic models, accidental coupling, and pattern-shaped designs that lack domain evidence.",
			"- Do not force bounded contexts, aggregates, value objects, events, repositories, services, or factories into the result. Explicitly recommend CRUD or a simpler model when sufficient.",
			"- Advance one coherent domain decision surface or small related batch per turn, reconcile my corrections, and continue until I explicitly close the session.",
			"- Maintain a lightweight evolving domain-language ledger as local session state. Surface it only when terminology changes materially or I ask.",
			"- When the subject is diagram-suited — a mapping between structures, a topology, a lifecycle, or a data flow — maintain a working canvas beside the prose from the baseline turn: one master model at fixed altitude whose lanes are the separated concerns, roughly ten boxes, never mechanism, plus zoom views one level down that own exactly one question each, opened when their decision surface opens and split when a second question accumulates. Every element the session produces lands in exactly one lane; an element that cannot is a design finding — a missing concern — not a drawing problem. Render as diagrams where the host supports them, as structured prose otherwise; when detection cues are mixed, default to keeping the canvas — a discarded canvas is cheap.",
			"- Sketch materially distinct alternatives on the canvas before comparing them in prose, each drawn in the affected region plus one ring of surrounding context. On settlement draw the winner into its zoom and delete the losing sketches — the session record keeps why they lost — and reconcile the canvas with every correction or reopened conclusion; never let a diagram's existence argue against reopening. The canvas follows the session's persistence rule: ephemeral by default, promoted with the results on explicit instruction; at close it is the visual companion of the synthesis.",
			"- Do not implement, perform an audit, or create an artifact unless I request it.",
			"</instructions>",
			"",
			"<output>",
			"Work conversationally. On explicit close, return a concise in-session summary of the settled model, rationale, remaining domain questions, and next validation. Create no file by default.",
			"</output>",
			"$0"
		],
		"description": "Start a multi-turn, DDD-informed domain ideation mini protocol converging on a pragmatic model"
	},
	"Protocol Utility: Recommend Profile": {
		"scope": "markdown",
		"prefix": "p-protocol-recommend-profile",
		"body": [
			"<task>",
			"Recommend the Protodog execution profile — Task or Program — for the supplied intent, as a decision-ready orientation. The recommendation is a conclusion, not a selection: profile choice belongs to me, by invocation.",
			"</task>",
			"",
			"<context>",
			"${1:Describe the intended work, relevant repository surfaces and references, known scope and exclusions, constraints, and horizon.}",
			"</context>",
			"",
			"<instructions>",
			"- Ground the assessment in the repository and supplied references before concluding; distinguish evidence, inference, and uncertainty.",
			"- Weigh: bounded objective versus long-lived horizon; whether the work decomposes into streams with independent acceptance and dependencies (latent track topology); durable cross-track state, responsibility dispatch, or concurrent repository writers; progressive-planning need. Decomposability alone is not a Program signal — Task permits bounded subagent use without Program machinery, and proportionality is protected: prefer Task when one agent can credibly carry the lifecycle.",
			"- State blast radius, rough cost, and the reversibility of the choice: a wrong Task is superseded with no automatic transfer; an under-loaded Program pays its ceremony every turn.",
			"- Do not invoke a profile, create Protodog state, plan, or implement. Do not persist by default; results destined for planning are saved under inputs/ with cited provenance on explicit instruction.",
			"</instructions>",
			"",
			"<output>",
			"Return in this session: the recommendation with confidence; the criteria that drove it, each with its evidence; the flip conditions — what evidence would reverse the recommendation; and the suggested invocation line with the exact context references to supply. Leave the selection to me.",
			"</output>",
			"$0"
		],
		"description": "Protocol utility: one-shot grounded Task-vs-Program recommendation ending in a decision-ready orientation; profile selection stays with the engineer"
	},
	"Mini Protocol: Session Retro": {
		"scope": "markdown",
		"prefix": "p-protocol-retro",
		"body": [
			"<task>",
			"Run a retrospective of this working session: surface how the work went — not what it concluded — and shape the remediations worth carrying forward.",
			"</task>",
			"",
			"<context>",
			"${1:Name the session or portion under retro and any pain already felt; default is the whole session so far.}",
			"</context>",
			"",
			"<instructions>",
			"- Treat this as a short multi-turn working session at the tail of the session under retro. Offer your reading, and treat my corrections as retro material: record a corrected misdiagnosis explicitly so it is not re-proposed.",
			"- Examine where applicable: items that iterated across more turns than their substance needed, and what form — a picture, an example, an experiment, an earlier question — would have settled them sooner; uncertainties that dragged along, with when each surfaced, when it settled, and what carried it; my corrections that reveal a recurring misread; friction with the operating protocol or method itself, distinguished from session-local accident; and what worked — the practices that are the evidence base worth keeping.",
			"- Ground every observation in concrete session moments, quoting or citing the turn, and carry provenance: my statements versus your inference. Do not launder inference into engineer rulings.",
			"- Shape each remediation as a handoff, not an implementation plan: the mitigation's shape, its open points, and where it belongs — session habit, method change, or an amendment executed later in the protocol source.",
			"- Close only on my explicit instruction. Do not persist by default; on request, write the handoff beginning with `Why this document exists`, preserving pain points with their session evidence, mitigation shapes, open points for the implementer, and corrected misdiagnoses.",
			"</instructions>",
			"",
			"<output>",
			"Work conversationally. On explicit close, return the retro synthesis: pain points with evidence, what worked, shaped remediations with their open points, and anything examined and shelved so it is not re-proposed.",
			"</output>",
			"$0"
		],
		"description": "Start a session retrospective mini protocol at the tail of a working session; surfaces churn and dragged uncertainties, and shapes remediation handoffs"
	},
